  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "John David inherited leadership of a grocery business begun by his father, Joe, with a Woolloomooloo corner store. He became chief executive after his father died in 1972. The enterprise had progressed from neighbourhood shops to a wholesale warehouse and an organised support network for independent retailers. David modernised distribution, enlarged the liquor operation and invested in ordering technology.\n\nWith Coles and Woolworths squeezing smaller competitors, David pursued the scale needed to preserve their buying power. He absorbed AG Campbells and wholesalers in several states, secured the Australian IGA franchise and floated Davids during the following decade. The push brought costly store acquisitions and financial strain alongside growth. Metro Cash & Carry took control in 1998, and the renamed Metcash later concentrated again on wholesale supply.",
    "html": "<p>John David inherited leadership of a grocery business begun by his father, Joe, with a Woolloomooloo corner store. He became chief executive after his father died in 1972. The enterprise had progressed from neighbourhood shops to a wholesale warehouse and an organised support network for independent retailers. David modernised distribution, enlarged the liquor operation and invested in ordering technology.</p>\r\n<p>With Coles and Woolworths squeezing smaller competitors, David pursued the scale needed to preserve their buying power. He absorbed AG Campbells and wholesalers in several states, secured the Australian IGA franchise and floated Davids during the following decade. The push brought costly store acquisitions and financial strain alongside growth. Metro Cash &amp; Carry took control in 1998, and the renamed Metcash later concentrated again on wholesale supply.</p>\r\n",
